    <briefdescription>
<para>Expectations are checks whose outcomes are counted and reported. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para><computeroutput>t.expect (condition)</computeroutput> checks a condition, counts the result, and reports it; when the check fails, the test case continues, so that all its checks are evaluated and reported in one run.</para>
<para>The condition is one of:<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>a function comparator, such as <computeroutput>mt::eq (actual, 42)</computeroutput>, or a combination of them, which reports the values of its operands;</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>an exception check, such as <computeroutput>mt::throws (...)</computeroutput>;</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>any other value that converts to <computeroutput>bool</computeroutput> in a condition, such as <computeroutput>x == 1</computeroutput>, a pointer, or a <computeroutput>std::optional&lt;int&gt;</computeroutput>, which reports only the location of the failed check.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para>The values that would make the check pass by mistake do not compile: the string literals and the other character arrays and pointers, the function names, the pointers to functions and to members, and the wrappers of a <computeroutput>bool</computeroutput> (<computeroutput>std::optional&lt;bool&gt;</computeroutput> and the smart pointers to <computeroutput>bool</computeroutput>), whose condition tells whether a value is present, not what it is. The scoped enumerations do not compile either, since only a cast converts them. Write the comparison instead, such as <computeroutput>mt::eq (flag.value (), true)</computeroutput>. With the deprecated definitions enabled, all of these are accepted, with a deprecation warning, except the wrappers of a <computeroutput>bool</computeroutput> and the scoped enumerations.</para>
<para>A message can be appended with <computeroutput>&lt;&lt;</computeroutput>, with any values that can be written, for example <computeroutput>t.expect (x == 2) &lt;&lt; "x is " &lt;&lt; x;</computeroutput>. The check is reported at the end of the statement, with the message, or, without one, with the condition.</para>
<para><simplesect kind="par"><title>Example</title><para></para>
</simplesect>
<programlisting filename=".cpp"><codeline><highlight class="normal">ts.test<sp/>(</highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&quot;Check<sp/>the<sp/>values&quot;</highlight><highlight class="normal">,<sp/>[]<sp/>(</highlight><highlight class="keyword">auto</highlight><highlight class="normal">&amp;<sp/>t)<sp/>{</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/></highlight><highlight class="keywordtype">int</highlight><highlight class="normal"><sp/>x<sp/>=<sp/>1;</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/>t.expect<sp/>(mt::eq<sp/>(x,<sp/>2));</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/>t.expect<sp/>(x<sp/>==<sp/>2)<sp/>&lt;&lt;<sp/></highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&quot;x<sp/>is<sp/>&quot;</highlight><highlight class="normal"><sp/>&lt;&lt;<sp/>x;</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">});</highlight></codeline>
</programlisting></para>
<para>Both checks fail; the human-readable reporter displays:</para>
<para><programlisting filename=".console"><codeline><highlight class="normal">•<sp/>Check<sp/>the<sp/>values</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/>✗<sp/>FAILED<sp/>(main.cpp:13,<sp/>1<sp/>==<sp/>2)</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/>✗<sp/>x<sp/>is<sp/>1<sp/>FAILED<sp/>(main.cpp:14)</highlight></codeline>
</programlisting>  </para>
    </detaileddescription>
